# The installed packages older than their recipe in the tree already in
# place, which of them the signed binhost index already in the cache covers,
# and the update that makes this system current, into the update document.
# Its readers are kdos-sysupdated (the system update Discover shows, and the
# AppStream file it serves), the update tray (the Discover notifier's KDOS
# plugin) and kdos-notice. It downloads nothing: no tree, no index, no source.
# A newer tree and its index arrive only when somebody asks — Discover's
# "Check for updates", which kdos-sysupdated answers with
# `kdos update check --fetch`, or `kdos update fetch` typed at a shell — so no
# machine reaches the network on a schedule of its own. Nightly and NOT at
# midnight: the job reads the whole ports tree and every package entry.
#
# -s 8h is the missed-run rule and it is snooze's, not ours: a machine that was
# ASLEEP at 04:17 runs the job ONCE when it wakes, if it wakes within eight
# hours of the slot. Not once per missed occurrence — a laptop shut for a
# fortnight would otherwise run fourteen of them at breakfast.
#
# A machine that was OFF is a different case and this line does not cover it: a
# snooze started at boot begins looking one second from now, so the slot it
# slept through is simply tomorrow's. Reaching back needs `-t FILE`, and a
# timefile only the job itself may touch.
update-check  -H 4 -M 17 -s 8h  --  kdos update check --out /var/lib/kdos/update.json
